{{NZelectorateShortDesc|current=yes|type=general|region=Auckland}}

'''Mount Roskill''' is a parliamentary [[New Zealand electorates|electorate]] in [[Auckland]], New Zealand, returning one Member of Parliament (MP) to the [[New Zealand House of Representatives]]. [[Phil Goff]]<ref>[http://www.parliament.nz/en-NZ/MPP/MPs/MPs/1/f/4/50MP981-Goff-Phil.htm New Zealand Parliament – Phil Goff MP]</ref> of the [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]] held the seat from the {{NZ election link|1999}} until he resigned from Parliament on 12 October 2016<ref>{{cite news | url=https://www.gazette.govt.nz/notice/id/2016-ps5847 | title=Notice of vacancy in seat in House of Representatives | work=New Zealand Gazette | date=13 October 2016 | access-date=30 October 2016}}</ref> after contesting and being elected [[Mayor of Auckland]] on 8 October 2016 in the [[2016 Auckland mayoral election|2016 mayoral election]]. His resignation necessitated a byelection in this electorate which was won by [[Michael Wood (New Zealand politician)|Michael Wood]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://m.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=11724629|access-date=9 October 2016|title=Phil Goff elected Mayor of Auckland|date=8 October 2016|work=The New Zealand Herald}}</ref>

[[Carlos Cheung]] of the [[New Zealand National Party]] currently holds the seat after defeating Wood in the [[2023 New Zealand general election]].

==Population centres==
{{maplink|from=New Zealand Electorates/2020/General/Mount Roskill.map|frame=yes|text=Map of boundaries at the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020]] and [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023]] general elections}}
Mount Roskill is located on the western side of the [[Auckland isthmus]], bordering the [[Manukau Harbour]]. It is anchored around the suburbs of [[Mount Roskill]], [[Three Kings, New Zealand|Three Kings]], [[Hillsborough, Auckland|Hillsborough]], [[Waikōwhai]], and a large section of [[Balmoral, New Zealand|Balmoral]]. The {{NZ election link|2008}} boundaries added in [[Lynfield, New Zealand|Lynfield]] and [[New Windsor, New Zealand|New Windsor]] at the expense of [[Onehunga]], which returned to the {{NZ electorate link|Maungakiekie}} electorate after being cut out in 1999. At the 2020 redistribution it gained New Windsor from {{NZ electorate link|New Lynn}} at the expense of Royal Oak, which moved to {{NZ electorate link|Maungakiekie}}.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://vote.nz/assets/Boundary-Review/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2020.pdf |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2020 |date=17 April 2020}}</ref> In the 2025 boundary review, the electorate would gain the suburb of [[Blockhouse Bay]] from {{NZ electorate link|New Lynn}} while transferring [[Balmoral, New Zealand|Balmoral]], [[Sandringham, New Zealand|Sandringham]] and [[Wesley, New Zealand|Wesley]] to {{NZ electorate link|Mt Albert}}.<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2025 |url=https://elections.nz/assets/pagecomponent-file-files/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2025-WEB.pdf |isbn=978-0-473-75233-0 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=16 September 2025 |page=19 |date=8 August 2025 }}</ref>

The Mount Roskill electorate is working class and multi-ethnic, with a high Pacific Island and Asian population compared to the national average. 52.8% of the population were born overseas - fourth highest percentage in the country, and 48.6% are of Asian ethnicity - the highest percentage in the country. The electorate also contains the highest proportion of New Zealanders that are practitioners of [[Hinduism in New Zealand|Hinduism]] and [[Islam in New Zealand|Islam]], as of 2021.<ref name="parllib">{{cite web| url= https://www.parliament.nz/en/mps-and-electorates/electorate-profiles/mt-roskill-electorate-profile/ |title=Mt Roskill - Electorate Profile |publisher= Parliamentary Library |date= 31 July 2020 |access-date= 16 September 2025}}</ref>

==History==
The [[1996 New Zealand census]] showed population growth in the north and west of Auckland, necessitating the redistribution of electorates for the {{NZ election link|1999}}. The existing {{NZ electorate link|New Lynn}} seat was renamed {{NZ electorate link|Titirangi}}, with its boundaries shifted to fall in between [[Auckland City|Auckland]] and [[Waitakere City|Waitakere]] cities. The eastern side of the [[New Lynn]] residential area was amalgamated with the population excess of {{NZ electorate link|Epsom}}, the southern half of {{NZ electorate link|Owairaka}} seat (which was itself renamed {{NZ electorate link|Mount Albert}}) and the western end of {{NZ electorate link|Maungakiekie}} to form a new seat. Named Mount Roskill, it was the first new seat drawn since the introduction of [[Mixed Member Proportional]] voting three years previous.{{citation needed|date=November 2014}}

So far there have been three MPs for Mount Roskill, two from the [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]], and one from the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party.]] Labour's [[Phil Goff]] was the first representative, having previously held New Lynn, another electorate in Auckland, and {{NZ electorate link|Roskill}}, an electorate covering much of the same area as Mount Roskill.
After Goff was elected [[Mayor of Auckland]] in October 2016, a by-election date was set for 3 December 2016.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.radionz.co.nz/news/political/315349/mt-roskill-becomes-national-labour-battleground |title=Mt Roskill becomes National-Labour battleground |work=Radio NZ |author=Demelza Leslie |date=11 October 2016 |access-date=16 October 2016}}</ref>  Labour candidate [[Michael Wood (New Zealand politician)|Michael Wood]] won the by-election with more than half the votes.{{citation needed|date=November 2023}}

[[Carlos Cheung]] won the seat for the first time for the National Party in the 2023 New Zealand general election, defeating former Minister and incumbent MP Michael Wood with a 22-point swing.{{citation needed|date=November 2023}}

